To build a file-sharing service with password protection, you need both a **Frontend** (a webpage for users to upload and download) and a **Backend** (a server to handle the files and verify passwords securely).

Here is a complete, minimal, and working application using **Node.js** and **Express**.

---

### Step 1: Set up the project

1. Make sure you have [Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) installed.
2. Open your terminal or command prompt, create a new folder, and initialize it:
   ```bash
   mkdir file-share
   cd file-share
   npm init -y
   ```
3. Install the required dependencies:
   ```bash
   npm install express multer bcrypt
   ```
   * *express*: Web framework.
   * *multer*: Handles file uploads.
   * *bcrypt*: Hashes passwords securely.

---

### Step 2: Create the Server (`server.js`)

Create a file named `server.js` and paste the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const multer = require('multer');
const bcrypt = require('bcrypt');
const crypto = require('crypto');
const path = require('path');
const fs = require('fs');

const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// Ensure upload directory exists
const uploadDir = path.join(__dirname, 'uploads');
if (!fs.existsSync(uploadDir)) {
  fs.mkdirSync(uploadDir);
}

// Multer storage configuration
const storage = multer.diskStorage({
  destination: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, uploadDir),
  filename: (req, file, cb) => cb(null, `${Date.now()}-${file.originalname}`)
});
const upload = multer({ storage });

// In-memory file database (resets when the server restarts)
const fileDB = new Map();

app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true }));
app.use(express.static('public'));

// Endpoint 1: Upload a file
app.post('/upload', upload.single('file'), async (req, res) => {
  if (!req.file || !req.body.password) {
    return res.status(400).send('File and password are required.');
  }

  // Generate a random link ID
  const id = crypto.randomBytes(6).toString('hex');
  // Hash the password for security
  const hashedPassword = await bcrypt.hash(req.body.password, 10);

  fileDB.set(id, {
    path: req.file.path,
    originalName: req.file.originalname,
    password: hashedPassword,
  });

  const downloadLink = `${req.protocol}://${req.get('host')}/download/${id}`;
  res.send(`
    <h2>File uploaded successfully!</h2>
    <p>Share this link with your friends:</p>
    <a href="${downloadLink}">${downloadLink}</a>
    <br><br>
    <a href="/">Upload another file</a>
  `);
});

// Endpoint 2: Show download form
app.get('/download/:id', (req, res) => {
  const fileData = fileDB.get(req.params.id);
  if (!fileData) {
    return res.status(404).send('File not found or link expired.');
  }

  res.send(`
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
    <head><title>Download File</title></head>
    <body style="font-family: sans-serif; max-width: 400px; margin: 50px auto;">
      <h2>Download: ${fileData.originalName}</h2>
      <form action="/download/${req.params.id}" method="POST">
        <label>Enter Password:</label><br><br>
        <input type="password" name="password" required style="width: 100%; padding: 8px;" /><br><br>
        <button type="submit" style="padding: 10px 15px; cursor: pointer;">Download</button>
      </form>
    </body>
    </html>
  `);
});

// Endpoint 3: Verify password and download
app.post('/download/:id', async (req, res) => {
  const fileData = fileDB.get(req.params.id);
  if (!fileData) {
    return res.status(404).send('File not found.');
  }

  // Compare submitted password with hashed password
  const isValid = await bcrypt.compare(req.body.password, fileData.password);
  if (!isValid) {
    return res.status(401).send('<h3>Incorrect password. <a href="javascript:history.back()">Try again</a></h3>');
  }

  // Send file to browser for download
  res.download(fileData.path, fileData.originalName);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Server running at http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

---

### Step 3: Create the Upload Frontend (`public/index.html`)

1. Inside your project folder, create a directory called `public`.
2. Inside `public`, create a file named `index.html`:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Password Protected File Share</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      max-width: 450px;
      margin: 60px auto;
      padding: 20px;
      border: 1px solid #ddd;
      border-radius: 8px;
    }
    input {
      width: 100%;
      margin: 8px 0 16px 0;
      box-sizing: border-box;
      padding: 8px;
    }
    button {
      background-color: #007bff;
      color: white;
      border: none;
      padding: 10px 15px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h2>Upload a File</h2>
  <form action="/upload" method="POST" enctype="multipart/form-data">
    <label>Choose File:</label>
    <input type="file" name="file" required />

    <label>Set Download Password:</label>
    <input type="password" name="password" placeholder="Enter password" required />

    <button type="submit">Upload & Get Link</button>
  </form>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### Step 4: Run the App

1. Start your server:
   ```bash
   node server.js
   ```
2. Open your browser and go to: `http://localhost:3000`
3. Upload a file, type a password, and submit. You will receive a unique link.
4. Open the link (even in an incognito window) and verify that you can only download the file after entering the right password.

---

### How to share it over the Internet

Right now, it only runs on your local computer (`localhost`). To share links with friends outside your home Wi-Fi:

* **Quickest way for testing:** Use [ngrok](https://ngrok.com/).
  * Run `npx ngrok http 3000`. It will give you a public URL (e.g., `https://xyz.ngrok-free.app`) that anyone can open.
* **Permanent way:** Deploy the project to a free or low-cost hosting platform such as [Render](https://render.com), [Railway](https://railway.app), or [Fly.io](https://fly.io). *(Note: For a production app, replace the in-memory `Map` with a real database like PostgreSQL or SQLite so links persist when the server restarts).*

